import type { ResearchPost } from './types';

export const november07ResearchPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"how-should-a-bdr-team-measure-time-to-first-qualified-conversation","title":"How should a BDR team measure time to first qualified conversation?","summary":"A stage-based method for measuring the delay between lead arrival and the first qualified conversation, with clear definitions and limits.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"Time to first qualified conversation is useful only when the start event, the qualification rule, and the business-hour clock are defined before the sample is drawn.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/how-should-a-bdr-team-measure-time-to-first-qualified-conversation.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how a BDR team should measure the time between a lead arriving and the first conversation that meets the team's qualification standard. The unit is one lead, followed from the moment it enters the tracked queue to the moment a qualified conversation is recorded. The measure is elapsed time, reported in business hours and calendar days. The scope excludes the buyer's later decision and the revenue that follows. The definition should be written before the sample so the result cannot be shaped after the fact.","The question matters because a fast first touch is not the same as a fast qualified conversation. A team can respond within minutes and still take weeks to reach the person who can describe the problem. Measuring only the first touch rewards speed at the front of the process while hiding delay in research, routing, and scheduling. A two-stage measure shows where the wait actually sits. It also gives the team a more honest basis for a service promise."]},{"heading":"Why first-touch metrics mislead","paragraphs":["First-touch time is easy to calculate because most systems record the send or call. That convenience makes it a common dashboard metric, even when it says little about pipeline quality. A lead can receive a fast automated reply and then wait days for a human who understands the account. The buyer experiences the whole wait, not the first timestamp. A useful measure follows the buyer's experience through the handoffs.","Another problem is that first touch can be gamed by sending a low-value message to stop the clock. The message may satisfy the metric without advancing the conversation. A qualified-conversation measure is harder to game because it requires a real exchange and a documented qualification. It also ties the measure to the outcome the team is trying to produce. That makes it a better input for coaching and capacity planning."]},{"heading":"Definitions and unit","paragraphs":["Define the start event as the moment the lead becomes visible in the tracked queue with enough information to work. If the lead arrives in a shared inbox and sits unread for two days, that waiting time should count because the team could have seen it. Define the end event as the first conversation that meets the qualification standard, not the first meeting booked. A booked meeting can be rescheduled or cancelled, while a qualified conversation has already produced evidence. The definition should name who records the end event.","Write the qualification standard before measuring. It may require a confirmed problem, a relevant role, a stated timeline, and a next step. Keep the standard short enough to apply consistently. If the standard changes during the observation window, split the sample into before and after. Mixing two standards makes the result hard to interpret. The report should state which standard was in force for each group."]},{"heading":"Data sources","paragraphs":["Use the queue system for the start time, the CRM for the qualification record, and the calendar for the conversation time. Compare the three sources before trusting any one of them. A lead may appear in the queue on one date and in the CRM on another because of a manual import. Record the discrepancy rather than smoothing it away. The source of each timestamp should be visible in the export.","Capture the owner at each stage, the routing history, and the channel of the first qualified conversation. These attributes explain why some leads move faster than others. A lead routed to a specialist may take longer but produce a better conversation. Without the attributes, the team can only see an average that hides the useful variation. The export should include the account tier and the lead source as well."]},{"heading":"Calculation method","paragraphs":["Calculate the elapsed time from the start event to the end event for each lead. Then break the total into stages such as intake, research, first attempt, response, and scheduling. Report the median and the upper range for the total and for each stage. The median shows the typical experience, while the upper range shows the leads that may need a different process. Count the leads that exceeded the service window as a separate measure.","Choose one clock and apply it consistently. Business-hour time excludes nights and weekends, while calendar time includes them. A team that works across time zones needs a written rule for whose hours apply. Report both measures when the difference changes the conclusion. A lead that waits over a weekend may look slow in calendar days and normal in business hours. Showing both prevents a misleading comparison."]},{"heading":"Reading the pattern","paragraphs":["Look for the stage with the largest share of total delay. If research is the slowest stage, the source list or the brief may be too demanding for the account tier. If first attempt is slowest, the assignment or the capacity may be the problem. If scheduling is slowest, the calendar may be the constraint rather than the BDR. Each pattern points to a different repair. Name the stage in the report so the repair has a target.","Segment the results by lead source, account tier, and owner. A pattern that appears only in one segment is easier to fix than a queue-wide shift. A high-tier lead that takes longer may be expected, while a low-tier lead with the same delay may be a routing problem. Compare like with like before drawing a conclusion. The segment view should use the same definitions as the total."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The measure describes one period and may not predict a busy month. The timestamps depend on people updating the record, and some updates may be late or missing. The qualification standard may be applied differently by different owners. The team should review a sample of records to confirm that the end event matches a real conversation. Include the observation window and the sample size in the report.","Do not use the measure to rank individuals without accounting for assignment and complexity. A BDR who receives the hardest accounts may show longer times for reasons outside their control. Use the result to improve routing, research support, and capacity. Then review individual performance with the context of the queue. A short note on assignment difficulty makes the comparison fairer."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the median time, the stage breakdown, the share of leads past the service window, and the sample size. Add a short note on the clock, the qualification standard, and any missing data. Show the stage view before the owner view because the stage view is usually more actionable. Keep the report short enough to discuss in a working session. A table with the counts is often enough.","Use the findings to change one part of the process at a time. A new research brief, a routing rule, or a protected scheduling block can each be tested. Measure the same stages after the change and compare the results. The goal is a shorter, more predictable path to a qualified conversation, not a perfect dashboard. A follow-up measurement after the change shows whether the repair worked."]},{"heading":"From measurement to a decision","paragraphs":["A measurement becomes useful when the team agrees on one decision it will inform. If the delay is concentrated in research, the decision may be to narrow the source list for low-tier accounts. If the delay is in scheduling, the decision may be to hold protected calendar blocks. Write the decision and the expected change before the next measurement. That prevents the report from becoming a monthly artifact with no action.","Keep the definition stable while the team tests the change. If the qualification standard or the start event changes at the same time, the comparison will not be clear. Run the new process for a full cycle before judging it. Then compare the same stages and the same service window. A measured change is more convincing than a general sense that the queue feels faster."]},{"heading":"Turning the measure into a service promise","paragraphs":["A measure becomes a service promise only when the team can explain what it includes and what it leaves out. A promise of a qualified conversation within five business days should state whether the clock starts at form submission or at queue entry. It should also state what happens when the buyer is unavailable or the lead is incomplete. The team can then design the process around the promise instead of discovering its limits after a complaint. A clear promise is easier to keep and easier to revise.","Review the promise against the measured distribution before publishing it internally. If only half the leads meet the proposed window, the promise will create pressure to cut corners. Adjust the window or the process, not the definition of a qualified conversation. Share the distribution with the team so the target feels grounded in real work. A promise that the team helped shape is more likely to be honored during a busy week."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"what-evidence-makes-a-prospect-list-defensible-under-review","title":"What evidence makes a prospect list defensible under review?","summary":"A field-level evidence standard for prospect lists, with source rules, review tests, and honest limits.","cluster":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A prospect list is defensible when each fit field is tied to a named source and access date, and the list states which accounts rest on weaker evidence.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/what-evidence-makes-a-prospect-list-defensible-under-review.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what evidence a prospect list needs before a manager or client can trust it. The unit is one account record and the fields that determine whether the account belongs on the list. The test is whether a reviewer can open the source, confirm the value, and understand the date it was checked. The scope includes fit fields, source notes, and review status, but excludes outreach performance. The record should stand on its own without the researcher present.","The question matters because a list can look precise while resting on assumptions. A company name and a size range may come from a directory that was last updated two years ago. A trigger may come from a news article that describes a different subsidiary. The reviewer needs a way to see which fields are strong and which are weak. Without that view, the list becomes a source of false confidence."]},{"heading":"Why lists fail review","paragraphs":["The most common failure is a missing source. The value looks reasonable, but no one can tell where it came from or when it was checked. A second failure is a source that does not support the field. A company page may list a service line, but not the size or region the list requires. A third failure is a field that mixes fact and judgment without a label. Each failure is easy to fix if the record is designed to expose it.","Another failure is coverage bias. A list built from one directory may over-represent companies that pay for inclusion or that happen to use a particular naming convention. The reviewer may not see the gap because the list is internally consistent. A defensible list states its source mix and known gaps. It also explains why the chosen sources are appropriate for the question."]},{"heading":"The evidence record","paragraphs":["Each account row should carry the fit fields, the source for each field, and the date the source was checked. For fields that come from a conversation, record the speaker, the date, and the channel. For fields that come from a public source, record the URL and the specific page or section. If a field is inferred, label it as inferred and name the basis. The record should distinguish the value from the evidence behind it.","Add a confidence value for the row as a whole, but do not let it replace the field-level notes. A row can be strong on company type and weak on the trigger. The reviewer needs that detail to decide how much verification to do before outreach. A single confidence score hides the weak link. The row should make the weakest field easy to find."]},{"heading":"Source rules","paragraphs":["Prefer primary sources for the fields that matter most. A company registration or official filing is stronger than a directory summary. A company page is stronger than a third-party profile for information the company controls. When only a secondary source is available, record what it summarizes and why the primary source was not used. That note tells the reviewer whether the evidence is likely to hold.","Check the access date, not only the publication date. A page can change without a new publication date, and a directory can update quietly. Record the date the researcher actually viewed the source. For fast-changing fields such as leadership or service lines, set a shorter review window. For stable fields such as region, a longer window may be acceptable. The review window should match the rate of change."]},{"heading":"Review test","paragraphs":["Ask a reviewer who did not build the list to pick a sample of accounts and reconstruct the fit decision from the record alone. The reviewer should be able to state the value, the source, and the date for the key fields. If they have to call the researcher, the record is not defensible. The reviewer should mark each field as confirmed, weak, or missing. That marking is more useful than a single pass or fail.","Include at least one account that the reviewer is likely to challenge. A sample of only the strongest records will not test the standard. The weak records show whether the list labels uncertainty or hides it. After the review, compare the fields that failed across accounts. A pattern in one field may point to a source problem or a definition problem. The repair should follow the pattern."]},{"heading":"Reading the gaps","paragraphs":["A missing source in one field may be an isolated omission. A missing source across many accounts in the same field suggests the source is hard to use or the field is poorly defined. A field that is always inferred may need a different source or a narrower definition. Do not simply add more fields to the form. Fix the field that the review shows is weak.","A high share of weak trigger evidence may mean the list is being built too early in the research cycle. A high share of weak size evidence may mean the directory does not cover the segment. The pattern should guide the next source choice. It should also guide the conversation with the outreach owner about which accounts need a verification call first."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A defensible list is not a guaranteed list. The evidence can be accurate and the account can still be a poor fit for reasons the record does not capture. The reviewer may interpret a field differently from the researcher. The source may change after the access date. State these limits in the list summary so the reader knows what the record can and cannot prove. A list with clear limits is easier to use responsibly.","Do not treat the presence of a URL as proof of quality. A link can point to a page that no longer contains the value or that never supported the claim. The reviewer should open the source for material fields. The record is a map to the evidence, not a substitute for reading it. A short spot check is enough for most lists, but the check should actually happen."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of accounts, the share with a source for every key field, the share with a source older than the review window, and the known coverage gaps. Add a short note on the source mix. These counts show whether the list is ready for outreach or needs another pass. They are more useful than a simple count of rows. A small table by field makes the weak areas visible.","Use the list with the confidence notes attached. The outreach owner should know which accounts need verification before a personalized message and which can be contacted with the current evidence. Record what the outreach reveals so the researcher can update the list. The feedback loop improves the next batch and keeps the evidence current. A list that is never corrected will drift away from the market."]},{"heading":"Making the standard repeatable","paragraphs":["Turn the evidence standard into a short checklist that the researcher uses before saving a row. The checklist should name the required fields, the acceptable source types, and the review window. Keep it short enough to use during real work. A long policy that nobody opens will not improve the list. The checklist should be visible in the same tool where the list is built.","Review the checklist after each batch and remove fields that do not change a decision. Add a field only when a reviewer has asked for it more than once. That keeps the record focused on the evidence that matters. The standard should evolve from actual review findings, not from a wish list. Over time, a simple standard applied consistently beats a detailed one applied occasionally."]},{"heading":"Keeping the list current between reviews","paragraphs":["A list does not stay defensible on its own after the first review. Set a short maintenance rhythm that checks the fields most likely to change and leaves the stable fields alone. Assign an owner for the check and a deadline for corrections. Keep a log of what changed so the next review starts with context. A small maintenance habit is cheaper than rebuilding the list from scratch.","Use the outreach feedback to update the list between formal reviews. When a buyer corrects a company detail or a trigger, record the correction and the source. If the same correction appears across several accounts, the original source may be wrong or out of date. Update the source rule, not just the individual row. That turns field feedback into a better list for the next batch."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"how-much-context-does-a-partner-introduction-need-to-survive-handoff","title":"How much context does a partner introduction need to survive handoff?","summary":"A handoff study of partner introductions, with the minimum context fields, a cold-read test, and clear limits.","cluster":"Partnership systems","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A partner introduction survives handoff when the record names the people, the reason, the ask, the commitments, and the evidence, and a cold reader can repeat the next step.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/how-much-context-does-a-partner-introduction-need-to-survive-handoff.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how much context a partner introduction needs so that the account owner can act without another internal conversation. The unit is one introduction request from the moment it reaches the owner to the moment the first external message is sent or the request is declined. The measure is whether the owner can identify the people, the reason, the ask, and the commitments from the record alone. The scope excludes the partner's response and the outcome of the relationship. The test should be applied before the introduction is sent.","The question matters because introductions are a handoff between people who may not share a workflow. The researcher knows the partner and the reason for the connection, while the account owner knows the buyer and the relationship. If the context does not cross that gap, the owner either delays or writes a generic message. Both outcomes waste the partner's time and weaken the relationship. A small amount of well-chosen context prevents most of that loss."]},{"heading":"Why introductions lose context","paragraphs":["Introductions often arrive as a forwarded email with a name and a short note. The note may explain who the partner is but not why the connection matters now. The owner then has to search for the account, read the history, and infer the ask. That work is easy to postpone. By the time the owner returns to it, the partner may have moved on. The context loss is not a person's failure; it is a missing record.","A second loss happens when the researcher assumes the owner shares the same mental model. The researcher may know that a partnership is strategic because of a recent announcement, while the owner sees only a company name. Without the evidence, the owner cannot judge the value. The introduction needs enough context to make the reason visible, not enough to reproduce the entire research file. The balance is the design problem."]},{"heading":"The minimum context fields","paragraphs":["The record should name the partner contact, their role, their company, and the relationship to the team. It should name the account owner and the buyer contact, or state the rule for choosing one. It should describe the opportunity in one or two sentences with a specific reason. It should state the ask and the timing. These fields are the minimum for an owner to decide whether to act.","Add what the partner has already agreed to and what remains open. This is the field most often missing, and it changes the tone of the first message. A partner who has agreed to a call is different from a partner who has only expressed interest. The record should also link to the evidence for any material claim. A short evidence field is enough when the links are clear."]},{"heading":"The cold-read test","paragraphs":["Give the record to an owner who has not spoken with the researcher and ask them to describe the introduction in their own words. The owner should be able to state who the partner is, why the connection matters, what they are being asked to do, and what has been promised. If any of those four items is missing, the record needs work. The test should happen before the introduction is sent, not after a failed attempt.","Record the questions the owner asks during the test. Those questions are the gaps that the next request should close. If the same question appears across different introductions, the template needs a new field or a clearer definition. If the question is specific to one partner, a short note may be enough. The test is cheap and repeatable, and it turns a vague standard into a concrete one."]},{"heading":"Context that does not belong","paragraphs":["More context is not always better. A long company history, a full list of possible use cases, and a detailed commercial analysis can bury the ask. The owner may read the first paragraph and postpone the rest. The record should include only the context that changes the decision or the message. Everything else can be linked. A concise record is more likely to survive a busy day.","Sensitive information needs a separate rule. Do not put personal details, private commercial terms, or internal assessments into an introduction record that may be shared widely. Record the minimum needed and link to the controlled source. Follow the team's access policy and any contractual requirement. A record that exposes more than the decision requires creates risk without adding value."]},{"heading":"Review and feedback","paragraphs":["After the introduction, ask the owner two questions: was the record enough, and what would have made it faster? Keep the answers short and store them with the request. Over time, the answers show which fields are used and which are ignored. A field that nobody reads may be unnecessary. A field that everyone asks for should be required.","Review a small sample of introductions each month and check whether the record matches the message that was actually sent. A mismatch may mean the owner had context the record did not capture. That is a signal to update the record, not to blame the owner. The review should improve the handoff and the template. It should also recognize introductions that were declined for a good reason."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A complete record cannot guarantee a good introduction. The partner may not be a fit, the timing may be wrong, or the buyer may not be interested. The record can only make the decision easier and the message more accurate. The cold-read test measures comprehension, not judgment. A reader can understand the request and still decide not to act. State that limit when the results are shared.","The sample may be small and may not represent every partner type. A strategic partner and a referral partner need different context. Report the types included and the ones left out. Do not generalize from a few introductions to the whole program. The goal is a better handoff for the cases reviewed, then a wider test when the pattern is clear."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of introductions reviewed, the share that passed the cold-read test, and the fields most often missing. Add one anonymized example of a strong record and one weak one. The examples make the standard concrete and give the team something to copy. Keep the report short enough to discuss in a working session. The count behind each share should be visible.","Use the findings to change one part of the template or the training. If the ask is often missing, add a required ask field. If the commitments are unclear, add a short status field. Then test a new sample and compare. The goal is an introduction that the owner can act on without a meeting. A follow-up test after the change shows whether the repair worked."]},{"heading":"From handoff to relationship","paragraphs":["The introduction record should end where the relationship begins. Once the owner has made contact, the record can be archived and the account record becomes the source of truth. Keep a link to the introduction so the history is visible, but do not maintain two copies of the same conversation. A clean end point prevents the record from becoming stale.","Ask the owner to note the outcome in one line: connected, declined, or no response. That line helps the researcher understand which introductions are worth repeating. It also gives the partnership team a realistic view of the pipeline. The record is not a scorecard for the researcher. It is a learning tool for the next handoff."]},{"heading":"What to do when the owner declines","paragraphs":["A decline is useful information when it is recorded with a reason. The owner may decline because the partner is not relevant, the timing is wrong, or the ask is too large. Record the reason and share it with the researcher without turning the exchange into a debate. A clear reason helps the next request avoid the same problem. It also protects the relationship with the partner because the team can respond honestly.","Do not treat a decline as a failure of the brief by default. The brief may be complete and the answer may still be no. Review a small sample of declines to see whether the reasons cluster around one field or one partner type. If the same issue appears repeatedly, change the brief or the qualification rule. If the reasons are varied, the process may be working as intended."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"when-does-a-shared-inbox-stop-scaling-as-a-bdr-intake-channel","title":"When does a shared inbox stop scaling as a BDR intake channel?","summary":"A diagnostic for shared inbox intake, with volume signals, routing tests, and a staged transition to a tracked queue.","cluster":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A shared inbox stops scaling when the volume of messages exceeds the team's ability to route each one to a named owner within the service window, even though the inbox still looks active.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/when-does-a-shared-inbox-stop-scaling-as-a-bdr-intake-channel.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks when a shared inbox should stop being the primary intake channel for BDR work. The unit is one inbound message that requires a response, a route, or a record. The signal is the gap between arrival and ownership, not the unread count alone. The scope includes replies, referrals, event follow-ups, and general inquiries that reach the shared address. It excludes internal chat and direct messages unless they follow the same path.","The question matters because a shared inbox is cheap to start and hard to retire. It works well for a small team with a common context. As volume and variety grow, the inbox becomes a place where work is seen but not owned. The team may add labels and rules, but the underlying problem remains. A clear transition point helps the team change the process before the backlog becomes a service failure."]},{"heading":"Why unread counts mislead","paragraphs":["An unread count measures attention, not ownership. A message can be read by three people and still have no owner. The count may fall to zero at the end of the day because someone archived a batch, even though the work is still open. A low unread count can coexist with a long response delay. The team needs a measure that follows the message to a named owner and a next action.","Another misleading signal is the number of labels. A complex label system can make the inbox look organized while hiding the fact that messages are not moving. Labels describe categories, not commitments. They do not show who will respond or when. A useful diagnostic counts the messages that have an owner and a dated next step. That count is usually smaller than the unread count and more honest."]},{"heading":"The scaling signals","paragraphs":["The first signal is a rising share of messages that are older than the service window without an owner. The second is a growing number of messages that are routed to the same person because no one else has the context. The third is a rise in duplicate replies or missed replies as more people touch the inbox. Each signal points to a different constraint, but all three show that the inbox is no longer a reliable routing layer.","A fourth signal is the time managers spend triaging instead of reviewing quality. When a manager becomes the default router, the process depends on one person's attention. A fifth signal is the number of messages that arrive through channels the inbox does not cover, such as direct messages or event apps. If the team is already copying those into the inbox by hand, the intake model is under strain. Count the manual copies before deciding."]},{"heading":"Routing test","paragraphs":["Run a routing test on a sample of recent messages. For each message, record the arrival time, the first owner, the time the owner was assigned, and the time the first response was sent. Then calculate the share that reached an owner within the service window. A message that was answered by whoever happened to see it does not count as routed unless that person is recorded as the owner. The test should use the team's real service window.","Include messages that were closed without a response, such as spam or vendor pitches. Those still consume attention and should be counted in the volume, but they should not be mixed into the response measure. Separate the categories before calculating the result. A high share of non-work messages may mean the address is too public. A high share of work messages with no owner is the stronger scaling signal."]},{"heading":"Staged transition","paragraphs":["The transition should start with a clear front door, not with a new tool. Define which messages belong in the tracked queue and which can stay in the inbox. Add a simple routing rule for each category and a named owner for the queue. Keep the inbox as the public address so the buyer experience does not change abruptly. The queue becomes the system of record behind the inbox.","In the first stage, route only the highest-volume or highest-risk category. Measure the routing time and the owner coverage for that category before expanding. In the second stage, add the next category and adjust the rules. Do not move everything at once, because the team will lose the ability to see which change helped. A staged transition also gives the team time to train on the new record."]},{"heading":"What to keep in the inbox","paragraphs":["Some work should stay in the inbox because it is genuinely conversational and short-lived. A quick scheduling confirmation or a one-line answer may not need a queue item. The rule should distinguish a conversation from a task. If the message creates a commitment, a follow-up, or a record, it belongs in the tracked system. If it closes in one reply with no further action, the inbox can handle it.","Keep the inbox clean enough that a new message is visible. Archive or move messages once they are routed. Do not use the inbox as an archive of completed work. The team should be able to look at the inbox and see only what still needs attention. A clean inbox is a better signal of new work than a folder with thousands of read messages."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The diagnostic describes the current process and may not predict a sudden spike. The service window may be informal, and different people may apply it differently. The sample may miss messages that never reached the inbox because they went to a personal address. State these limits when the results are shared. A small sample of real messages is still more useful than an opinion about the inbox.","Do not use the result to conclude that the team needs a new tool immediately. A routing rule and a named owner can solve many problems without a migration. A tool change adds cost and disruption, and it can hide a process problem rather than fix it. Test the simpler changes first. The diagnostic should point to the constraint, not to a product."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the sample size, the share of messages routed within the window, the share with a named owner, and the median time to first response. Add the volume by category and the number of manual copies from other channels. These counts show whether the inbox is still a reliable front door. Keep the report short and use the same definitions as the routing test.","Use the findings to choose one change for the next month. A new triage time, a clearer category rule, or a named queue owner can each be tested. Measure the same signals after the change and compare. The goal is not an empty inbox but a reliable path from arrival to ownership. A follow-up test shows whether the path actually improved."]},{"heading":"From inbox to owned work","paragraphs":["The transition succeeds when the team can answer three questions without opening the inbox: what arrived, who owns it, and when the next action is due. If those answers live in the queue, the inbox has become a front door rather than a backlog. If they still live in individual memory, the transition is incomplete. The test is the team's ability to hand off work without forwarding a thread.","Keep the buyer experience stable during the change. The public address, the acknowledgment message, and the response expectation should stay the same. The internal routing can change without the buyer noticing. That is the point of a staged transition. The team improves ownership without asking the buyer to learn a new process."]},{"heading":"Signals that the transition is working","paragraphs":["A transition is working when the share of messages with a named owner rises and the median time to first response falls without an increase in duplicate replies. The unread count may stay similar because new work keeps arriving. That is why ownership and response time are better measures than an empty inbox. Track the same categories before and after the change. A stable or improving trend across two periods is stronger than a single good week.","Ask the team whether the queue is easier to hand off than the inbox. A process that looks better in a report but requires more explanation in practice is not working. The handoff test is whether a teammate can pick up an item without opening a thread. If they can, the transition has changed the operating model. If they cannot, the queue may be a new label on the same old problem."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Administrative Services and Facilities Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/administrative-services-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"can-a-stopped-sequence-be-recovered-without-resetting-the-account","title":"Can a stopped sequence be recovered without resetting the account?","summary":"A recovery test for stopped sequences, with re-entry conditions, evidence checks, and a clear rule for when to start over.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A stopped sequence can be recovered without resetting the account when the original reason is documented, a new fact justifies re-entry, and the next message acknowledges the earlier context.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/can-a-stopped-sequence-be-recovered-without-resetting-the-account.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks whether a stopped outreach sequence can be recovered without treating the account as new. The unit is one sequence that ended without a clear close, such as no response, a timing deferral, or a contact change. The test is whether the team can restart with the earlier context intact and a documented reason for the new touch. The scope excludes accounts that were explicitly disqualified or asked not to be contacted. Those should stay closed unless a new request arrives.","The question matters because resetting an account erases useful history and can repeat a message the buyer already ignored. It also makes the team look disorganized. A recovered sequence should use what the team learned, not pretend the earlier work did not happen. The difference is visible to the buyer in the relevance of the next message. A thoughtful re-entry is more likely to earn a reply than a fresh generic sequence."]},{"heading":"Why teams reset by default","paragraphs":["Resetting is easy because most tools start a new sequence from a clean record. The old sequence is closed, the new one begins, and the activity history becomes a long list that no one reads. The team may reset because the original reason was never recorded. Without a reason, the only safe-looking action is to start over. The record design is therefore part of the recovery problem.","A second reason is that re-entry requires judgment. The team has to decide whether the earlier context is still true and whether a new fact justifies another touch. That takes more effort than pressing start. The effort is worth it because a recovered account carries relationship history that a new account does not. The team should make the judgment visible rather than avoid it."]},{"heading":"Recovery conditions","paragraphs":["Define the conditions that allow re-entry. A new fact, such as a funding event, a leadership change, a product launch, or a relevant public statement, is a strong condition. A scheduled revisit date with a clear question is another. A change in the team's offer or proof is a third. The condition should be specific enough that a reviewer can confirm it. A general feeling that enough time has passed is not a condition.","The condition should also match the original stop reason. A timing deferral can be reopened on the agreed date. A no-response sequence needs a new reason to believe the buyer's situation changed. A contact departure needs a new buyer and a new path. A fit rejection should stay closed unless the fit criterion itself has changed. Matching the condition to the reason prevents a random restart."]},{"heading":"Evidence and history","paragraphs":["Before re-entry, read the original sequence and the account record. Confirm what was sent, what the buyer said, and what the team concluded. Check whether any commitment was made that is still open. A promise to follow up on a specific date should be honored before a new message is sent. The re-entry note should link to the original sequence so the next owner can see the history.","Record the new fact and its source. If the fact is a public announcement, link to it and note the date. If it is a conversation, record who said it and when. The evidence should be strong enough to explain why the team is reaching out now. A vague reference to a change in the market will not help the buyer understand the timing. The note should make the reason easy to repeat."]},{"heading":"Writing the re-entry message","paragraphs":["The message should acknowledge the earlier context in one sentence without apologizing for the gap. For example, it can refer to the earlier conversation and the question that was left open. Then it should introduce the new fact and explain why it matters to the buyer. The message should end with a specific, low-friction next step. It should not repeat the entire original pitch.","Tone matters more than length. A re-entry message that sounds like a fresh sequence will feel generic. A message that references the earlier conversation shows that the team kept a record. Keep the references accurate and avoid implying a relationship that does not exist. If the buyer never replied, say that the team is following up with a new reason. Honesty is more persuasive than familiarity."]},{"heading":"Ownership and review","paragraphs":["Assign the recovered account to an owner who can access the original context. If the original owner is unavailable, the new owner should review the history before sending. The review should confirm the re-entry condition, the evidence, and the message. A second person can catch a claim that the record does not support. The review does not need to be long, but it should happen before the send.","Record the review in the account so the team can see who approved the re-entry. If the account is recovered and then stalls again, the team should review the second stop with the same discipline. Repeated recovery without a new fact is a sign that the account should be closed or moved to a longer nurture cycle. The record should make that pattern visible."]},{"heading":"When to start over","paragraphs":["Starting over is appropriate when the earlier context is no longer relevant or cannot be trusted. A merger, a complete change in the buyer's business, or a new offer that addresses a different problem can all justify a fresh approach. Even then, keep the old record linked so the history is not lost. A clean start should be a documented choice, not a default.","Do not start over simply because the team has a new template. A new template can be used within the existing context if the earlier messages were relevant. Resetting the account to use a new sequence adds noise without adding insight. The decision should depend on the buyer and the evidence, not on the team's internal preference. That distinction keeps the outreach focused on the buyer."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A recovered sequence cannot guarantee a reply. The new fact may be relevant and the timing may still be wrong. The buyer may have changed roles or lost interest. The record can only show that the team had a reason to reach out and used the earlier context. State that limit when the recovery is reviewed. A thoughtful attempt can still fail for reasons outside the team's control.","The sample of recovered accounts may be small and may not represent every segment. A high-touch enterprise account and a small business account need different recovery rules. Report the types included and the ones left out. Do not generalize from a few cases to the whole queue. The goal is a better decision for the accounts reviewed, then a wider test when the pattern is clear."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of stopped sequences reviewed, the share with a documented stop reason, the share with a valid re-entry condition, and the share that received a response after recovery. Add one anonymized example of a strong recovery and one weak one. The examples make the standard concrete. Keep the report short enough to use in a team session.","Use the findings to improve the stop note and the re-entry rule. If many stops lack a reason, fix the template before adding more recovery steps. If many recoveries lack a new fact, tighten the condition. Then measure the next cycle and compare. The goal is a queue that can restart accounts with context and evidence, not a queue that resets by habit."]},{"heading":"A recovery decision the team can defend","paragraphs":["The recovery decision should be defensible to a manager, a buyer, and the original owner. A manager should see the reason and the evidence. A buyer should recognize the earlier context and understand why the team is writing now. The original owner should recognize the history and the commitments. If any of those three cannot follow the decision, the record is incomplete.","Write the decision in one short paragraph and link it to the account. That paragraph becomes the reference if the recovery is questioned later. It also helps the next owner if the account moves again. A clear decision is more valuable than a long sequence of messages. The team should aim for a record that explains the choice, not just the activity."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission, Complying with the Telemarketing Sales Rule","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-telemarketing-sales-rule"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"how-should-a-bdr-manager-audit-call-notes-for-decision-quality","title":"How should a BDR manager audit call notes for decision quality?","summary":"An audit method for call notes, with decision criteria, sample design, calibration, and a realistic view of limits.","cluster":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A call-note audit measures decision quality when it asks whether a reader can identify the buyer's situation, the evidence, and the next action without hearing the call.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/how-should-a-bdr-manager-audit-call-notes-for-decision-quality.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how a BDR manager can audit call notes for the quality of the decision they support. The unit is one note attached to one call. The test is whether a reader who did not join the call can identify the buyer's situation, the evidence behind it, and the next action. The scope includes discovery, qualification, pricing, and disqualification calls. It excludes internal meetings and general account updates that are not tied to a buyer conversation.","The question matters because note quality is often judged by length or completeness rather than usefulness. A long note can repeat the transcript without helping anyone decide. A short note can be excellent if it names the right facts and the next step. The audit should reward the second and coach the first. That requires a standard tied to decisions, not to word count."]},{"heading":"What a useful call note contains","paragraphs":["A useful note records the buyer's situation in specific terms, the evidence the buyer provided, and the open questions that remain. It names the next action and the owner, and it links to any document or recording that supports a material claim. It separates what the buyer said from the BDR's interpretation. These elements are enough for most calls, and they can be written in a short paragraph plus a few fields.","The note does not need to capture every sentence. It needs to capture what changes the decision. A call that confirms a budget cycle and a decision maker may need only those two facts and a follow-up date. A call that reveals a compliance constraint may need a link to the buyer's explanation. The standard should be proportional to the call's purpose, not uniform across every conversation."]},{"heading":"Audit criteria","paragraphs":["Use four criteria: situation, evidence, interpretation, and next action. Situation asks whether the note explains the buyer's problem in concrete terms. Evidence asks whether the note shows where the key facts came from. Interpretation asks whether the BDR's conclusion is separated from the facts. Next action asks whether the note names what happens next and who owns it. Each criterion can be scored as present, partial, or missing.","Add a fifth criterion for handoff readiness when the account will move to another owner. The note should give the next owner the context they need without a call. If the account stays with the same BDR, the handoff criterion can be marked not applicable. The audit should use the criteria that fit the call type. A pricing call and a discovery call do not need the same fields."]},{"heading":"Sample design","paragraphs":["Sample notes across BDRs, call types, account tiers, and outcomes. A sample drawn only from won deals will overstate quality because successful calls tend to have more context. Include calls that ended without a next step and calls that were later reversed. These cases often reveal the weakest notes. Record the sample size and the selection rule before reviewing.","Keep the sample stable across rounds so the comparison is meaningful. If the team changes its call types or its CRM fields during the audit, start a new sample. A small sample reviewed consistently is more useful than a large sample reviewed once. The manager should be able to repeat the audit without rebuilding the method each time."]},{"heading":"Calibration","paragraphs":["Have a second reviewer score the same sample before the full audit. Compare the scores and discuss the differences. If the disagreement comes from a vague criterion, rewrite the definition. If it comes from a different reading of the call, record the context and move on. The goal is a shared standard, not perfect agreement. A short calibration session improves the reliability of the results.","Calculate a simple agreement measure and report it with the audit. A score with low agreement looks precise but means little. Recalibrate when the call type, the offer, or the reviewer group changes. The calibration should focus on the criteria that cause the most disagreement. Those are the definitions that need the most work."]},{"heading":"Giving feedback","paragraphs":["Feedback should name the criterion that was weak and show a short example from the same call. Instead of saying the note was vague, point to the sentence that lacks a source and suggest the question to ask next time. Keep the feedback to one or two items so the BDR can act on it. A long list of corrections is harder to use than a focused note.","Separate coaching from evaluation. The audit is primarily a tool for improving notes and the template. If the results are used in performance review, the manager should account for call difficulty and assignment. A BDR with complex accounts may write longer notes for good reasons. The score should be one input, not the whole judgment. The purpose is better decisions, not a ranking."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The audit measures the note, not the call. A well-written note can describe a call that was poorly handled, and a weak note can follow a good conversation. The reviewer may interpret the buyer's words differently from the BDR. The sample may miss calls that were never logged or notes that were written days later. State these limits when the results are shared.","Do not use the audit to require more fields than the team will maintain. A short standard that people actually follow is better than a detailed form that gets skipped. The audit should make the note easier to write well, not harder. If a criterion is consistently missing, ask whether it is useful before adding another reminder."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the sample size, the criterion-level results, and the agreement measure. Add one or two anonymized examples at the high and low end. The examples make the standard concrete and give the team something to copy. Keep the report short enough to discuss in a working session. The criterion view is more actionable than a single average.","Use the findings to change one part of the process at a time. Improve the template, add a short training example, or adjust the call review. Then audit a new sample and compare the same criteria. The goal is a note that supports the next decision without a follow-up call. A measured change is more convincing than a general instruction to write better notes."]},{"heading":"Making the audit part of the rhythm","paragraphs":["The audit works best when it happens close to the calls, not months later. Add a short monthly sample to the existing review rhythm. Ask the BDR to self-score one note before the manager reviews it. The comparison shows where the BDR and the manager disagree about the standard. That conversation is often more useful than the score itself.","Keep the audit notes with the sample so the team can see how the standard evolved. If a criterion is removed, record why. If a new example is added, link it. A visible history makes the audit feel like a shared practice rather than a secret rating. Over time, the team internalizes the standard and the audit becomes lighter."]},{"heading":"Connecting note quality to coaching","paragraphs":["The audit should end with a small number of coaching actions, not a long list of defects. Pick the criterion that failed most often and write one example that shows the difference between a weak note and a strong one. Use that example in the next team session and in the template prompt. Then review a new sample after two weeks. A single focused change is easier to sustain than a full redesign.","Track whether the coaching changes the next sample rather than whether people remember the instruction. If the same criterion remains weak, the issue may be the template, the call flow, or the time available after a call. Ask the BDR what made the note hard to write. The answer may point to a tool or a scheduling problem rather than a skill gap. The audit should improve the conditions for good notes, not only the notes themselves. That distinction keeps the review focused on the system that produces the record."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"what-capacity-signal-should-trigger-a-new-bdr-hire-or-assistant","title":"What capacity signal should trigger a new BDR hire or assistant?","summary":"A capacity diagnostic that separates temporary backlog from a persistent gap, with thresholds, role scoping, and limits.","cluster":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","finding":"A new BDR hire or assistant is justified when a persistent capacity gap remains after process and scope changes, and the gap is concentrated in work that a defined role can own.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/what-capacity-signal-should-trigger-a-new-bdr-hire-or-assistant.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what signal should trigger a new BDR hire or assistant rather than another round of overtime. The unit is one work queue over a defined period. The signal is a persistent gap between demand and the capacity to complete qualified work without skipping review. The scope includes research, preparation, sending, reply handling, and CRM work that the team owns. It excludes revenue targets and headcount plans that are set without reference to the queue.","The question matters because hiring is slow and expensive, while overtime feels temporary and cheap. A team can spend months covering a structural gap with extra hours before anyone names it. A clear signal helps the manager act before quality falls. It also prevents a hire from being used to solve a process problem that a simpler change could fix. The decision should follow the evidence, not the loudest week."]},{"heading":"Why headcount ratios fail","paragraphs":["A ratio such as accounts per BDR ignores the variation in research depth, account complexity, and review requirements. One team may need more preparation time because its market is hard to research, while another may need more reply handling because its offer attracts questions. The ratio also hides the time spent on exceptions and internal coordination. A capacity gap can appear in a team that looks well staffed by the ratio.","Another weak signal is the backlog alone. A backlog can grow because of a temporary spike, a delayed import, or a process change. It can also grow because the team is avoiding a difficult segment. A backlog becomes a capacity signal only when it persists across several periods and cannot be cleared by a temporary change. The diagnostic should separate the temporary from the structural."]},{"heading":"Measuring demand and capacity","paragraphs":["Measure demand in units the team actually manages, such as accounts researched, messages prepared, or replies handled. Measure capacity in the same units using a representative period. Include the time for review, handoffs, and the exceptions that always appear. Do not use the best week as the baseline. A conservative baseline shows the gap more honestly and gives the plan a buffer.","Break the work into categories so the gap can be located. If the gap is in research, a specialist researcher or assistant may help. If the gap is in reply handling, a different role or a routing change may be the answer. If the gap is in review, adding senders will not solve it. The category view prevents a generic hire from being proposed for a specific constraint."]},{"heading":"Persistence test","paragraphs":["A gap is persistent when it appears in at least three consecutive periods after normal seasonal variation is considered. Check whether the gap shrinks when the team pauses low-value work or simplifies a step. If it does, the problem may be prioritization rather than capacity. If it remains, the team may have a real structural constraint. Record the periods and the changes tested.","Include the quality cost in the persistence test. If the team is meeting volume by skipping review or delaying CRM updates, the apparent capacity is borrowed from future work. A gap that produces rework is larger than the raw volume suggests. The test should use both the queue counts and a small quality sample. A capacity plan that ignores quality will fail in the next period."]},{"heading":"Thresholds and role scope","paragraphs":["Set a threshold that reflects the team's service promise and risk. For example, a persistent gap above a defined share of capacity for three periods may trigger a review for additional help. The threshold should be a prompt for a decision, not an automatic hire. The manager should still test scope and process options before adding a role. A threshold makes the conversation concrete without replacing judgment.","If the decision is to add help, define the work the new role will own. A researcher, an outreach assistant, and a queue coordinator solve different constraints. Write the categories, the review point, and the handoff before hiring. A clear scope makes onboarding faster and prevents the new person from becoming a general helper with no measurable contribution. The role should map to the gap found in the diagnostic."]},{"heading":"Alternatives before hiring","paragraphs":["Before adding a role, test a scope reduction, a template simplification, and a routing change. Each can free capacity without a new hire. A scope reduction may mean pausing a low-value segment for a quarter. A template simplification may remove fields that do not change a decision. A routing change may move replies to the person with the authority to answer. Measure the effect of each change before combining them.","Do not test several changes at once if the team needs to know which one worked. Run one change for a full cycle and compare the same measures. If the gap closes, the hire may not be needed. If it remains, the team has a stronger case for additional capacity. The sequence of tests is part of the evidence, not a delay."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The diagnostic describes the queue in one period and may not predict a new market or a major offer change. The time estimates depend on accurate logs and may miss informal work. The service promise may be informal and applied differently by different people. State these limits when the results are shared. A capacity decision made from a single week is a guess, not a plan.","Do not use the result to justify a permanent headcount before the role is defined. A new hire without a clear scope can add coordination work and make the queue harder to manage. The diagnostic should point to the constraint and the work category, not to a job title. The title should follow the work."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the demand, the capacity, the gap by category, the persistence across periods, and the changes tested. Add a short note on the service promise and the quality sample. These counts show whether the gap is structural and where it sits. Keep the report short enough for a planning meeting. A table by category is more useful than a single ratio.","Use the findings to choose one next step: reduce scope, change the process, or add a defined role. Then measure the same categories after the change. The goal is a queue that can meet its service promise without hidden overtime or skipped review. A follow-up measurement shows whether the decision worked. The plan should be reviewed again when the market or the offer changes."]},{"heading":"A decision the team can explain","paragraphs":["The capacity decision should be explainable to the team, the finance owner, and the buyer. The team should see which work the new role will take and how it will be reviewed. The finance owner should see the gap and the alternatives that were tested. The buyer should experience a stable service promise. If any of those three cannot follow the decision, the case is not complete.","Write the decision in one page and keep it with the queue data. Include the periods reviewed, the changes tested, the chosen option, and the measure that will show whether it worked. A short record prevents the same debate from returning every quarter. It also helps the next manager understand why the team is staffed the way it is. The goal is a capacity plan that survives a change in leadership."]},{"heading":"Reviewing the decision after the hire","paragraphs":["After a new role starts, measure the same categories that justified the hire. Check whether the gap closed, whether review time changed, and whether the quality sample held. A hire that adds volume but increases rework has not solved the capacity problem. A hire that frees the team to prepare better may improve quality even if volume grows slowly. The review should use the original measures, not a new set that makes the result look better.","Give the new role a defined review point and a way to raise constraints. A researcher who cannot get source access or an assistant who cannot see the queue will not deliver the expected capacity. The manager should check the handoff and the tools in the first month. If the role is not working, adjust the scope before adding another person. The goal is a capacity plan that learns from its own results."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Administrative Services and Facilities Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/administrative-services-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]}];
